# No rules, on purpose.
#
# The repository this was carved out of grew to 347 MB, of which 343 MB was
# generated output written beside the scripts that produced it -- invisible for
# weeks because .gitignore covered for it, and `git status` was clean the whole
# time it accumulated.
#
# Everything this repo generates goes to $SANDBOX. So anything that turns up
# here is a script writing where it should not, and an untracked file in
# `git status` is exactly the alarm you want. Adding a rule here would silence
# the one thing that tells you the rule is being broken.

# STILL NO RULES. Two hours after this file was written I ran `git add -A`
# without reading the status and committed five .pyc files -- the exact thing
# the empty ignore list exists to surface. The alarm worked; I bypassed it.
#
# The fix is not a rule. It is `python3 -B` in the verifiers' shebangs and
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE in build.sh, so the files are never written, plus
# reading `git status` before `git add`.
